1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,998
It is nineteen seventy seven.

2
00:00:01,998 --> 00:00:06,994
In a field in Ohio, a radio telescope is listening to the sky, completely unattended.

3
00:00:06,994 --> 00:00:09,659
Nobody is there to hear what it hears.

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:17,173
Days later, a volunteer sits at his kitchen table with a stack of paper printouts a technician dropped off on his way into town.

5
00:00:17,173 --> 00:00:20,361
He is not paid to do this anymore. He does it anyway.

6
00:00:21,499 --> 00:00:26,789
A few pages in, he stops. Six characters, in a column of numbers that should mean nothing.

7
00:00:26,789 --> 00:00:28,656
He picks up a red pen.

8
00:00:29,799 --> 00:00:35,521
He circles them. In the margin, without thinking too hard about it, he writes one word.

9
00:00:36,666 --> 00:00:40,370
Whatever his telescope heard that night has never been heard again.

10
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:45,757
Not once, in almost fifty years, across more than a hundred attempts to listen for it.

11
00:00:46,899 --> 00:00:51,811
This is not the only object science still can't explain. It isn't even the strangest one.

12
00:00:52,933 --> 00:00:55,731
There is a star that appeared to be building something.

13
00:00:55,731 --> 00:01:01,606
And a visitor from outside our solar system that broke the laws we thought applied to every rock in the sky.

14
00:01:02,733 --> 00:01:06,786
Real telescopes. Real data. Real scientists, still arguing about what they found.

15
00:01:06,786 --> 00:01:12,529
Ranked from the one we've mostly figured out, to the one we haven't figured out at all.

16
00:01:13,666 --> 00:01:15,155
You're watching mindwired.

17
00:01:16,299 --> 00:01:18,572
Objects astronomers can't explain.

18
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:20,727
Two thousand fifteen.

19
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:32,368
A NASA space telescope called Kepler has spent years staring at one patch of sky, watching over a hundred thousand stars for the tiny, regular flicker of a planet passing in front of them.

20
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:36,567
Kepler generates far too much data for scientists alone to check.

21
00:01:36,567 --> 00:01:42,981
So NASA hands some of it to ordinary volunteers — a project called Planet Hunters — to look at the charts by eye.

22
00:01:44,099 --> 00:01:45,739
One star stops them cold.

23
00:01:45,739 --> 00:01:50,003
Its brightness doesn't dip by the tiny, steady amount a planet would cause.

24
00:01:50,003 --> 00:01:55,906
It dips by up to twenty two percent — then climbs back, on no fixed schedule at all.

25
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:04,411
A planet, even one as big as Jupiter, blocks about one percent of a star's light, on a schedule you can set a clock to.

26
00:02:04,411 --> 00:02:10,904
To block twenty two percent, whatever passed in front of this star would have to be most of the star's own width.

27
00:02:12,033 --> 00:02:15,952
Nothing that large should be able to orbit a star without destroying itself.

28
00:02:15,952 --> 00:02:19,269
And whatever it was, it wasn't coming back on any schedule.

29
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:25,241
The star gets a name: KIC eighty four sixty two eight fifty two.

30
00:02:25,241 --> 00:02:31,945
Astronomers just call it Tabby's Star, after Yale astronomer Tabetha Boyajian, who led the team that published it.

31
00:02:33,066 --> 00:02:40,223
Her own first reaction, on record: "When they showed the star to me, we all thought it was a data glitch." It wasn't.

32
00:02:41,366 --> 00:02:53,722
A year later, astronomer Jason Wright and his team publish a paper asking a genuinely serious question: could this be what a megastructure under construction, built by another civilization, would actually look like from Earth?

33
00:02:54,866 --> 00:03:00,198
His own words, quoted directly: "Aliens should always be the very last hypothesis you consider.

34
00:03:00,198 --> 00:03:04,819
But this looked like something you would expect an alien civilization to build."

35
00:03:05,966 --> 00:03:08,887
That is not the same as saying he found one.

36
00:03:08,887 --> 00:03:12,392
It's a scientist saying: before we rule this out, let's actually check.

37
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:16,283
So other astronomers checked. Comet swarms. Dust rings.

38
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:22,127
A star that may have swallowed a planet ten thousand years ago and never quite settled down.

39
00:03:23,266 --> 00:03:33,297
Then, in twenty eighteen, a Kickstarter-funded telescope campaign catches a new dip happening live — and this time, they measure it in multiple colors of light at once.

40
00:03:34,433 --> 00:03:41,895
A solid object — a planet, a swarm of ships, a megastructure — blocks every color of light equally.

41
00:03:41,895 --> 00:03:45,822
This dimming didn't. It blocked blue light more than red.

42
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:50,853
That is the signature of dust. Not a solid object at all.

43
00:03:50,853 --> 00:03:56,683
Boyajian's own conclusion: "Dust is most likely the reason why the star's light appears to dim and brighten."

44
00:03:57,833 --> 00:04:07,394
Wright's own words on his idea, once the data came in: "This latest research rules out alien megastructures, but it raises the plausibility of other phenomena."

45
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:11,283
So: not aliens. That part is actually settled.

46
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:17,127
What's still not settled is which kind of dust, from what source, and why it's so extreme.

47
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:30,073
As of just this year, one new study finds a tentative — not yet confirmed — signal of a giant hidden planet in the system, one that could be stirring up comet debris and flinging it through the star's light.

48
00:04:31,199 --> 00:04:35,970
So this one makes our list at number three. Genuinely bizarre. Mostly explained.

49
00:04:35,970 --> 00:04:38,905
Still, right now, missing its last few pieces.

50
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:41,478
October nineteenth, twenty seventeen.

51
00:04:41,478 --> 00:04:48,706
On a mountain in Hawaii, a telescope built to watch for asteroids that could hit Earth catches something moving wrong.

52
00:04:49,833 --> 00:04:54,563
Astronomer Robert Weryk spots a faint streak in the data. He checks the night before.

53
00:04:54,563 --> 00:04:58,662
It's there too. Its path doesn't fit any normal orbit around our Sun.

54
00:04:59,799 --> 00:05:08,785
His own words: "It's just something that happened to run into us, and we were very lucky to have been operating the telescope that night and looking in that direction."

55
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:12,054
Within days, the math is undeniable.

56
00:05:12,054 --> 00:05:20,539
This object's path is bent just enough that it can only have come from outside our solar system entirely — and it's already leaving.

57
00:05:21,666 --> 00:05:26,603
It's named 'Oumuamua — Hawaiian for scout, or messenger from afar arriving first.

58
00:05:26,603 --> 00:05:31,540
And almost immediately, it starts doing things nothing in our solar system does.

59
00:05:32,666 --> 00:05:40,858
Its brightness swings by a factor of ten as it tumbles — meaning it's either wildly elongated, like a cigar, or flattened, like a pancake.

60
00:05:40,858 --> 00:05:44,134
Nothing in our own solar system is shaped like either.

61
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:46,733
Then it gets stranger.

62
00:05:46,733 --> 00:05:52,232
As it leaves, it speeds up slightly more than the Sun's gravity alone can explain.

63
00:05:52,232 --> 00:05:56,264
Real telescopes measure this precisely. It is not a rounding error.

64
00:05:57,399 --> 00:06:04,465
In a comet, that kind of push comes from ice boiling off the surface — a visible tail, a cloud of gas.

65
00:06:04,465 --> 00:06:08,318
Deep images and space telescopes look for exactly that, from up close.

66
00:06:09,466 --> 00:06:11,862
They find nothing. No tail. No gas.

67
00:06:11,862 --> 00:06:16,995
No dust cloud — even though it passed closer to the Sun than Mercury does.

68
00:06:16,995 --> 00:06:19,732
Something pushed it. Nothing visible was pushing it.

69
00:06:20,866 --> 00:06:31,393
One Harvard astronomer, Avi Loeb, co-authors a real peer-reviewed paper proposing an answer: what if it were thin enough, and light enough, to be pushed by sunlight itself — like a sail.

70
00:06:32,533 --> 00:06:40,751
His own paper lays out two options for what that sail could be: something we don't yet understand forming naturally out in space.

71
00:06:40,751 --> 00:06:43,609
Or, his words, debris from someone else's technology.

72
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:50,095
His later popular book argues harder for the second option than his own peer-reviewed paper ever did.

73
00:06:50,095 --> 00:06:53,249
Almost no other astronomer in the field agrees with him.

74
00:06:54,366 --> 00:07:05,364
In twenty nineteen, fourteen of the world's leading comet and asteroid experts review everything and conclude: every single observation is consistent with something entirely natural.

75
00:07:06,499 --> 00:07:08,596
But which natural explanation? Frozen hydrogen?

76
00:07:08,596 --> 00:07:14,188
A fragment of nitrogen ice, chipped off a Pluto-like world hundreds of millions of years ago?

77
00:07:14,188 --> 00:07:19,430
Scientists have proposed all of these — and are still arguing about all of them.

78
00:07:20,566 --> 00:07:23,583
Two more interstellar visitors have passed through since.

79
00:07:23,583 --> 00:07:29,239
Both behaved exactly like normal comets — visible tails, visible gas, nothing strange at all.

80
00:07:30,366 --> 00:07:35,019
Which makes the first one stand out even more. Number two on our list.

81
00:07:35,019 --> 00:07:40,005
A confirmed visitor from another star system, that did something we still can't fully explain.

82
00:07:41,133 --> 00:07:48,839
Which brings us back to Ohio, nineteen seventy seven, and the six characters a volunteer named Jerry Ehman circled in red pen.

83
00:07:49,966 --> 00:07:53,737
The telescope was called Big Ear, run out of Ohio State University.

84
00:07:53,737 --> 00:08:00,337
It scanned the same fixed strip of sky, over and over, as the Earth's own rotation swept it past different stars.

85
00:08:01,466 --> 00:08:07,047
Every ten seconds, the computer printed one character showing how strong any signal was.

86
00:08:07,047 --> 00:08:10,635
A quiet sky printed low numbers. This one didn't.

87
00:08:11,766 --> 00:08:16,110
The exact sequence was six, E, Q, U, J, five.

88
00:08:16,110 --> 00:08:23,495
The letter U meant the signal was roughly thirty times stronger than the background noise around it.

89
00:08:24,633 --> 00:08:30,263
It came in close to a very specific frequency — the exact wavelength hydrogen itself emits.

90
00:08:30,263 --> 00:08:36,597
The most common element in the entire universe, and the frequency every Seti search was built to watch.

91
00:08:37,733 --> 00:08:47,607
It lasted exactly as long as the telescope's fixed beam took to sweep across one point in the sky — seventy two seconds, rising and falling smoothly.

92
00:08:47,607 --> 00:08:52,727
Not a glitch. Not a flicker. A real object, in a real fixed spot.

93
00:08:53,866 --> 00:08:57,059
It came from the direction of the constellation Sagittarius.

94
00:08:57,059 --> 00:09:04,864
And it was never, not once, seen again — not that night, not the next night, not in nearly fifty years since.

95
00:09:05,999 --> 00:09:14,907
Astronomers have pointed some of the most sensitive radio telescopes on Earth at that exact spot at least six separate times across five decades, looking for it again.

96
00:09:16,033 --> 00:09:18,674
Every single search came back with nothing.

97
00:09:18,674 --> 00:09:25,843
As recently as twenty twenty two, the Breakthrough Listen project used two major observatories to search the region again.

98
00:09:25,843 --> 00:09:26,221
Nothing.

99
00:09:27,366 --> 00:09:32,421
In twenty seventeen, one researcher proposed a passing comet's hydrogen cloud could explain it.

100
00:09:32,421 --> 00:09:37,475
The scientists who actually found the signal reviewed the claim directly and rejected it.

101
00:09:38,599 --> 00:09:50,798
The most credible natural theory right now, published in twenty twenty four and twenty twenty five, is that a violent flare from a dead, collapsed star briefly lit up a cold cloud of hydrogen gas like a flashbulb.

102
00:09:51,933 --> 00:09:55,908
Even the scientists proposing it are careful about their own language.

103
00:09:55,908 --> 00:10:00,606
They call it a hypothesis. Not a discovery. Not an answer. A hypothesis.

104
00:10:01,733 --> 00:10:06,510
The telescope that heard it was torn down in nineteen ninety eight to make room for a golf course.

105
00:10:06,510 --> 00:10:07,767
It can never listen again.

106
00:10:08,899 --> 00:10:16,492
For decades afterward, whenever people asked the man who circled it in red pen what he thought it really was, he gave the same careful answer.

107
00:10:16,492 --> 00:10:20,288
He never claimed it was aliens. He just never ruled it out either.

108
00:10:21,433 --> 00:10:28,234
Nearly fifty years later, this is still, honestly, the strongest unexplained signal Seti has ever recorded.

109
00:10:28,234 --> 00:10:29,935
Not proof of anything.

110
00:10:29,935 --> 00:10:35,461
Just a real number, on a real printout, that nobody has ever explained.

111
00:10:36,599 --> 00:10:39,323
None of these three are proof of aliens.

112
00:10:39,323 --> 00:10:47,832
Every one of them, when scientists actually checked, pointed back toward something natural, something strange, or something we simply don't have an answer for yet.

113
00:10:48,966 --> 00:10:51,132
That's honestly the more interesting finding.

114
00:10:51,132 --> 00:10:54,021
The universe doesn't need aliens to be strange.

115
00:10:54,021 --> 00:10:59,075
It's strange enough on its own, and we're still only decades into really looking.

116
00:11:00,199 --> 00:11:05,384
Somewhere out there, right now, another telescope is recording something nobody has looked at yet.

117
00:11:05,384 --> 00:11:09,185
Statistically, most of what it finds will have a normal explanation.

118
00:11:10,333 --> 00:11:19,894
But every single mystery on this list started exactly that way — as one strange line, in one ordinary printout, that somebody almost didn't stop to look at twice.

119
00:11:21,033 --> 00:11:30,690
If ranked mysteries like this are your kind of thing, we've also broken down the sounds NASA actually recorded in deep space — real audio, ranked by how unsettling it actually gets.

120
00:11:30,690 --> 00:11:32,501
That one's on this channel too.

121
00:11:33,633 --> 00:11:37,846
Wait until you hear what NASA's own space sounds video has on it.

122
00:11:37,846 --> 00:11:42,384
Subscribe to mindwired, for the real science behind the strangest things in the universe.

